WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.100
Image segmentation.

2
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.198
Instead of boxes, the model classifies every single pixel.

3
00:00:06.198 --> 00:00:07.900
Let's see the two flavors.

4
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.733
The output of segmentation is a mask, the same height and width as the image.

5
00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:15.842
Each position holds a class.

6
00:00:15.842 --> 00:00:19.267
Here is a tiny label map, where the top row is cloud.

7
00:00:19.567 --> 00:00:22.200
And these two ships have exactly the same label.

8
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.500
There are two flavors, and interviewers love the distinction.

9
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.867
Semantic segmentation asks: what is this pixel?

10
00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:33.433
So all ships merge into one undifferentiated region.

11
00:00:33.733 --> 00:00:40.000
Instance segmentation also asks which object it belongs to, so touching ships get separate masks.

12
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:45.447
Semantic nets are encoder decoders, while instance models ride on detectors.

13
00:00:45.447 --> 00:00:46.733
Panoptic does both.

14
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:50.967
How does a semantic network produce a full size mask?

15
00:00:51.267 --> 00:00:54.233
Convolutions downsample to understand the image.

16
00:00:54.533 --> 00:01:01.633
Then a decoder upsamples back to full resolution to draw, with skip connections carrying fine detail across.

17
00:01:01.933 --> 00:01:04.533
Out comes a mask the same size as the input.

18
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:08.467
Here's semantic segmentation with DeepLab.

19
00:01:08.767 --> 00:01:13.033
The model outputs a score for each of twenty one classes at every pixel.

20
00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:16.800
Argmax picks one class id per pixel, giving the mask.

21
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.000
Then we count what percent of pixels each class covers.

22
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.433
Here's the right flavor for a real job.

23
00:01:23.733 --> 00:01:26.700
Every downstream job is garbage on a cloudy tile.

24
00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.067
So the first stage is a small U-Net that computes the cloud fraction.

25
00:01:31.367 --> 00:01:35.510
Tiles over forty percent cloud are skipped, and the number is logged.

26
00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.933
Nobody asked which cloud is which, so semantic was right.

27
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.015
Let's recap.

28
00:01:41.015 --> 00:01:43.367
Segmentation labels every pixel.

29
00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:46.967
Semantic merges identities, instance separates them.

30
00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.533
Semantic nets are encoder decoders.

31
00:01:49.833 --> 00:01:52.441
Pick by the question you're answering.

32
00:01:52.441 --> 00:01:53.333
Next up: OCR.
